Raw dev notes -- RAG Pipeline & Unexpected Website Adventures

Okay, so this past week has been a whirlwind. The core project is still about building out this RAG knowledge base pipeline. The idea is to take content from a couple of key sources – a main website (let's call it RB) and some chat logs – and process it so an AI can answer questions accurately and with proper attribution. It's all about making our internal knowledge more accessible and useful.

This sprint, I really dug into making the data ingestion more robust and comprehensive. For the RB site, I finally tackled the comments section. It was a mess, with pagination artifacts messing up the data, but I managed to clean that up and then split out all the answered comments into individual Q&A files, per article. This is huge for getting more granular, conversational data into the RAG system. On the chat log side, I retrofitted per-line provenance, which means we can trace exactly where an answer came from, and also filtered out a bunch of "no-answer" topics that were just noise. I even added a raw export for backup, just in case.

A big win was the RB backfill. We were missing a ton of articles – like, 886 of them! – so I built out the process to ingest those from the sitemap, bringing our article count from 861 to over 1700. That's a massive expansion of the knowledge base. To make sure this whole ingestion process doesn't fall over, I added auto-retries with backoff for transient failures (timeouts, rate limits) and even throttled fetches to avoid getting IP-blocked by the live site. Dealing with external APIs is always a dance. I also set up a 12-query retrieval regression battery and an adversarial manipulation-resistance set to make sure new content and changes don't break existing retrieval quality.

But then, things took a bit of a detour. Or maybe a parallel universe. Suddenly, I was deep into a full-blown website cloning and redesign effort for the RB site. I started with a full-fidelity clone, getting all the media, videos, dates, categories, SEO – everything – into a local WordPress instance. Then it escalated into a full redesign. I experimented with programmatic Elementor for the homepage, then moved to site-wide modern styling with Astra+CSS, and finally, the big leap: a full-site editing (FSE) block theme. This FSE work was intense – building out homepage sections, sortable archives, even adding native comment voting, TTS listen, and AI summaries. It felt like a whole separate project, but it's all about making the source content better, which ultimately feeds into the RAG system's quality.

The hardest part was definitely the unexpected scope of the website work. I went from thinking about text chunks and embeddings to wrestling with WordPress themes, Elementor, and FSE blocks. It was a steep learning curve, especially getting the full-fidelity clone right and then trying to modernize it. Also, those rate limits and IP blocks from the live site were a constant headache during data syncs; it's like the site was actively fighting me. Building the evaluation battery was also challenging, especially coming up with adversarial queries and ensuring the backfill didn't introduce regressions.

Honestly, it felt a bit overwhelming at times, jumping between RAG pipeline robustness and full-stack web development. But it was also incredibly satisfying to see the knowledge base grow so much and to build out a proper evaluation harness. And the website redesign, while a huge tangent, taught me a ton about modern WordPress development and how much control FSE offers. It's a good reminder that sometimes the path to improving one system involves a deep dive into its dependencies.

Next up: RAGify the comments more deeply and tackle the remaining website redesign backlog items.
